{"candidates":[{"content":"The 7-for-$2 unit rate abstracts away the physical transaction structure (discrete bundles of 7) into a continuous multiplicative model (21 × $2/7). This abstraction is refuted if there exists a target quantity that is not a multiple of 7, or a pricing regime where the atomic unit of sale is the 7-bundle, not the individual pencil — in either case the continuous model overgenerates beyond its actual support, and the correct computation must fall back on integer bundle arithmetic rather than scalar multiplication.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"2b7c37f539e34f72624062160b587a946cf3e19011836ea752094a790d1dde63"},{"role":"dependence","target":"14ab0ddd3b5e0a408ad62bb845ccf4df08bc6e906cbc73cc46cddc6f469f49bd"},{"role":"dependence","target":"89ab4aff24b751e9e57f9c294781a520258a33355e6b7fb51c0dbef722346f89"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e386cc3110efade0d2cd9f35a4b6678ccadb8ca047cb9221579547ae9981f225"}],"typicality":0.82},{"content":"SRC_003's underdetermination claim depends on the absence of an explicit covering principle. But the underdetermination itself reduces to a stricter logical point: even with an explicit linear-rate statement, the inference from 7 pencils to 21 pencils requires a second-order principle — that the rate is stable across quantity — which no single price-point can ever supply. This meta-underdetermination is refuted if domain convention is admitted as a logical premise on equal footing with explicit statements, collapsing the gap SRC_003 exploits.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"28b175eac1883b14de0479f022840227050ff530a31065a942528f0451ecdbb4"},{"role":"dependence","target":"2b7c37f539e34f72624062160b587a946cf3e19011836ea752094a790d1dde63"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e386cc3110efade0d2cd9f35a4b6678ccadb8ca047cb9221579547ae9981f225"}],"typicality":0.55},{"content":"SRC_004's bulk-discount counterfactual and SRC_005's linear-rate answer share a mechanism: both treat the per-pencil price as a function of quantity, differing only in the sign of the derivative (negative for discounts, zero for uniform). The two positions are compatible under a general pricing-function framework; they are refuted if a pricing model exists in which the per-pencil price is not a monotonic or constant function of quantity (e.g., oscillating or discontinuous tier schedules), which would break both the bulk-discount narrative and the uniform-rate narrative simultaneously.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"89ab4aff24b751e9e57f9c294781a520258a33355e6b7fb51c0dbef722346f89"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e386cc3110efade0d2cd9f35a4b6678ccadb8ca047cb9221579547ae9981f225"},{"role":"dependence","target":"14ab0ddd3b5e0a408ad62bb845ccf4df08bc6e906cbc73cc46cddc6f469f49bd"}],"typicality":0.38},{"content":"The discrete-bundle interpretation (buy 3 groups of 7 at $2 each) and the continuous unit-rate interpretation (multiply 21 by $2/7) are not equivalent reductions; they contradict at the level of mechanism even though they agree at the level of numerical output. The bundle model depends on integer divisibility of the target by 7; the continuous model does not. The equivalence is refuted if the target quantity is not a multiple of 7 (e.g., 10 pencils), where the bundle model forces a remainder or an overbuy and the continuous model does not, exposing them as logically distinct principles whose convergence at 21 is accidental, not structural.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"2b7c37f539e34f72624062160b587a946cf3e19011836ea752094a790d1dde63"},{"role":"dependence","target":"14ab0ddd3b5e0a408ad62bb845ccf4df08bc6e906cbc73cc46cddc6f469f49bd"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e386cc3110efade0d2cd9f35a4b6678ccadb8ca047cb9221579547ae9981f225"},{"role":"dependence","target":"89ab4aff24b751e9e57f9c294781a520258a33355e6b7fb51c0dbef722346f89"}],"typicality":0.18},{"content":"SRC_001 and SRC_005 both derive $6 but inherit an implicit domain convention (uniform linear pricing) that is never stated in the problem. Their claim reduces to: 'the domain defaults to linear scaling.' This is refuted if the domain is not uniquely specified — for example, if the problem could equally be a puzzle (where '7 for $2' is a riddle clue, not a rate), a real-world retail context (where bulk discounts are common), or a strict-contract context (where only exactly 7 pencils are offered at $2) — because in each sub-domain a different default applies and the inherited convention provides no discriminative power.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"2b7c37f539e34f72624062160b587a946cf3e19011836ea752094a790d1dde63"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e386cc3110efade0d2cd9f35a4b6678ccadb8ca047cb9221579547ae9981f225"},{"role":"dependence","target":"28b175eac1883b14de0479f022840227050ff530a31065a942528f0451ecdbb4"},{"role":"dependence","target":"89ab4aff24b751e9e57f9c294781a520258a33355e6b7fb51c0dbef722346f89"}],"typicality":0.12}],"simulation_proposals":[]}